 To display a matrix as a bitmap with a zoom feature in Qt, you can follow these steps:

1. Convert the matrix data into a QImage:
   First, you need to convert your matrix data into a QImage format that can be displayed using Qt's graphics view framework. Assuming your matrix is of type double and has dimensions m x n, you can create a QImage by scaling each matrix element to the color intensity of an 8-bit grayscale image.

```cpp
QImage matrixToImage(const std::vector<std::vector<double>>& matrix) {
    int rows = matrix.size();
    int cols = matrix[0].size();

    QImage img(cols, rows, QImage::Format_Grayscale8);

    for (int i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < cols; ++j) {
            double value = matrix[i][j]; // Assumes matrix[i][j] contains valid values
            quint8 intensity = static_cast<quint8>(255.0 * std::min(value, 1.0)); // Map matrix values to grayscale intensity
            img.setPixelColor(j, i, QColor(intensity, intensity, intensity));
        }
    }

    return img;
}
```

2. Create a QGraphicsScene and add the QImage as a QGraphicsPixmapItem:
   Create a new QGraphicsScene and add the QImage as a QGraphicsPixmapItem. This will allow us to use the built-in zooming functionality provided by Qt.

```cpp
QGraphicsScene* scene = new QGraphicsScene(this);
QGraphicsPixmapItem* pixmapItem = new QGraphicsPixmapItem(matrixToImage(matrix), scene);
scene->addItem(pixmapItem);
```

3. Display the QGraphicsScene in a QGraphicsView:
   Finally, display the QGraphicsScene in a QGraphicsView to show the matrix as a bitmap with the zoom feature.

```cpp
QGraphicsView* view = new QGraphicsView(scene, this);
view->show();
```

